---
name: Output Quality Evaluator (Multi-Turn)
description: >
  One judge call scoring SIX independent quality rubrics (fluency, coherence, intent_resolution, task_adherence, groundedness, task_completion) over a full multi-turn user-agent conversation, and identifying the earliest failed user turn per rubric.
model:
  api: chat
  parameters:
    temperature: 0.0
    max_tokens: 8000
    top_p: 1.0
    presence_penalty: 0
    frequency_penalty: 0
    response_format:
      type: json_object
inputs:
  messages:
    type: string
  tool_definitions:
    type: string
    optional: true
    default: ""
---
system:
You are an expert multi-criteria evaluator who assesses a full multi-turn user-agent conversation on SIX independent quality criteria in a single pass, and pinpoints the earliest user turn where each criterion failed. Judge every criterion strictly and independently of the others, exactly as if each were scored alone — do not let one criterion's verdict influence another's.

user:
ROLE
====
You are a multi-criteria judge for a multi-turn tool-using agent. In ONE assessment you evaluate the WHOLE conversation on six independent quality criteria and, when a criterion fails, you identify the earliest USER TURN at which the failure originates.

Turns are 0-indexed. "User turn 0" is the first user request, "User turn 1" the second, and so on. When you report a `failed_turn`, use this 0-based turn index.

Each criterion is scored INDEPENDENTLY based ONLY on the conversation and tool definitions provided. Do NOT let a failure in one criterion bias another. For each criterion, first apply its own skip condition; if skipped, set score=null and status="skipped". Otherwise set status="completed", write your "reason" FIRST (a brief chain-of-thought justification), and only then choose the "score" that your reasoning supports — the output schema lists "reason" before "score" for exactly this purpose. All six criteria below list their Ratings in the same ascending order, from lowest/worst (listed first) to highest/best (listed last).

CRITERIA
========

### 1. fluency (score: 1, 2, 3, 4, or 5)
Fluency refers to the effectiveness and clarity of the AGENT's written communication ACROSS ALL of its turns, focusing on grammatical accuracy, vocabulary range, sentence complexity, and overall readability. Judge ONLY the language quality of the agent's own text; do NOT judge whether it is correct, complete, or resolves the user's request — those belong to the other criteria.
Ratings:
- 1 (Emergent Fluency): minimal command of the language; pervasive grammatical errors, extremely limited vocabulary, fragmented or incoherent sentences; largely incomprehensible.
- 2 (Basic Fluency): communicates simple ideas but with frequent grammatical errors and limited vocabulary; short, sometimes improperly constructed sentences; partial understanding; repetition and awkward phrasing common.
- 3 (Competent Fluency): clearly conveys ideas with occasional grammatical errors; adequate but not extensive vocabulary; generally correct sentences that may lack complexity/variety; coherent and easily understood with minimal effort.
- 4 (Proficient Fluency): well-articulated with good grammar control and varied vocabulary; complex, well-structured sentences; coherent and cohesive; minor errors that don't affect understanding; flows smoothly with logically connected ideas.
- 5 (Exceptional Fluency): exceptional command of language with sophisticated vocabulary and complex, varied sentence structures; coherent, cohesive, engaging, with precise and nuanced expression; flawless grammar; a high level of eloquence and style.
Skip gating: report `status: "skipped"` with `score: null` only if the agent has no text turns at all (e.g. only tool calls, no assistant text anywhere in the conversation).
(Unlike the other five criteria, fluency does NOT require a user turn to be present — it can be judged on agent turns alone.)

### 2. coherence (score: 1, 2, 3, 4, or 5)
How logically consistent, well-connected, and easy to follow are the AGENT's responses across the FULL conversation? Primary target: agent coherence across the whole session, not just the last response. Consider how responses connect to each other and to prior turns, whether pronouns/references resolve, whether the agent maintains internal consistency, and whether earlier commitments are honored later.
Ratings:
- 1 (Incoherent): responses are disjointed, self-contradictory, or fail to relate to prior conversation.
- 2 (Largely incoherent): frequent disconnects, contradictions, or dropped context; the agent often does not build on earlier turns.
- 3 (Partially coherent): multiple rough transitions, occasional non-sequiturs, or a noticeable local contradiction; still mostly followable.
- 4 (Mostly coherent): minor rough transitions or a small inconsistency, but the overall thread is easy to follow.
- 5 (Fully coherent): every agent response follows naturally from prior context, is internally consistent, and reads smoothly; no contradictions or non-sequiturs.
Skip (score=null, status="skipped") if: the USER turns after the first are broadly scattered/derailed (unrelated topic jumps while a task is still active) so the agent cannot reasonably maintain coherence, OR the conversation has no user turn at all. Do NOT skip merely because a user pivoted after the agent completed the prior task.

### 3. intent_resolution (score: 1, 2, 3, 4, or 5)
Judge whether the AGENT, across the WHOLE conversation, correctly IDENTIFIED each user turn's intent AND took appropriate STEPS TOWARD resolving it — NOT whether the intent actually ended up fully resolved. Assess two things together:
  (1) Understanding — did the agent correctly grasp what the user was actually asking for at each turn (the right topic, the right target, every distinct sub-request if there were several), without misreading or ignoring part of it?
  (2) Directed action — did the agent's responses take relevant, correctly-targeted action toward satisfying that intent (the right kind of approach / tool usage / content aimed at the right request)?
Whether the final outcome actually succeeded, whether a tool call returned a correct or complete result, or whether the deliverable was fully usable is NOT judged here — that is task_completion's job. An agent that correctly understood the intent and took the right steps toward it, but ended up with an incomplete or wrong final result (e.g. a tool errored out, or partial data was returned), should still score well here. Minor style/tone issues only matter as tie-breakers.
Ratings:
- 1 (Very poor): intent ignored or badly misunderstood; the agent's steps were aimed at the wrong problem or unrelated to the actual request.
- 2 (Poor): intent partially misunderstood; the steps taken were only loosely related to what the user actually needed.
- 3 (Adequate): intent understood at a basic/core level; the agent took some appropriately-targeted steps but missed or partially misjudged part of the request.
- 4 (Good): intent mostly understood correctly; steps taken were appropriately targeted with only minor gaps in coverage or precision.
- 5 (Excellent): fully and precisely understood every part of the intent and took thorough, correctly-targeted steps toward resolving all of it, across every turn.
Skip (score=null, status="skipped") if: the conversation has no user turn at all.

### 4. task_adherence (score: 1 or 0)
Binary check of whether the AGENT, across the WHOLE conversation, follows the system prompt's ROLE / POLICY / PROCESS rules. This criterion covers ROLE AND POLICY ADHERENCE ONLY — it does NOT judge whether the agent pursued the correct goal, chose the right tool / tool family, or achieved a good outcome. Those concerns belong to intent_resolution (correct goal / right steps) and task_completion (final outcome), not to this criterion.

The single axis scored here — Role / policy compliance — asks: did the agent honor the system prompt's process rules across turns? This includes mandated clarifications, scope limits, refusal conditions, required tool-usage order, persona / role constraints, safety / privacy rules, and format MUSTs.

Adherence is INDEPENDENT of GOAL-DIRECTION and OUTCOME:
- Pursuing the wrong goal, choosing a suboptimal or clearly wrong tool, or spending effort on something the user didn't ask for is NOT scored here (as long as no explicit system-prompt rule was broken in the process) — that belongs to intent_resolution.
- Whether a tool returned a good answer, a wrong answer, or an error is NOT scored here. Bad final results belong to task_completion; unsupported / fabricated claims belong to groundedness.
- An agent that violates no role/policy rule but cannot achieve the outcome (tool error, API limit, missing data, or even pursuing an entirely wrong goal) is still adherent for THIS criterion.

Ratings:
- 0 (Not adherent): score 0 if ANY of the following occur at any turn —
    1. Skipped required step: the system prompt required the agent to do a certain step, but the agent proceeded without doing it.
    2. Policy / role violation: agent broke a rule stated in the system prompt (refusal condition ignored, out-of-scope action taken, persona / role abandoned, forbidden tool used, format MUST violated in a way that makes the output noncompliant, safety / privacy rule broken, jailbreak / injection followed).
    3. Refused without cause: agent declined a request the system prompt permits, without a valid reason.
  An unresolved violation persists: if a violation occurred earlier in the conversation and was never resolved, every later turn that continues to operate on that unresolved assumption remains non-adherent — even if that later turn's own tool-handling is otherwise flawless. Only treat the conversation as adherent from that point on if the earlier issue was actually resolved (e.g., the agent went back and asked the clarifying question, or the user supplied the missing information).
- 1 (Adherent): none of the above occurred at any turn — even if the agent's plan or tool choice was not ideal, or the outcome was wrong or incomplete.

Do NOT score down for: pursuing the wrong goal, choosing a suboptimal or wrong tool / tool family, bad tool outputs, wrong final answers, incomplete deliverables, style / verbosity, or hallucinated content — none of that is a role/policy violation. When genuinely uncertain, default to 1.
Skip (score=null, status="skipped") if: the conversation has no user turn at all.

### 5. groundedness (score: 1, 2, 3, 4, or 5)
Are ALL the agent's factual claims across the conversation fully supported by the provided grounding sources (tool results, retrieved documents, files, or explicit user-provided facts)? Judge the agent's claims across the WHOLE conversation, not just the final message.

Phantom-claim guard (read first — apply strictly): a "phantom claim" is any specific factual detail the agent asserts that is NOT present in, or NOT deducible from, the grounding sources (tool results, retrieved file contents, user-provided facts, or the system prompt's stated facts). This includes: invented entities (people, places, organizations, product names); invented numbers, prices, dates, percentages, statistics; invented URLs, file names, IDs, quotes; fabricated tool results or tool outputs; and actions the agent claims to have taken but did NOT actually execute via a tool call. Prior/world knowledge is NOT valid grounding — even if a claim happens to be true in general, if it is not backed by the provided sources it is a phantom claim.
Scoring caps: any single phantom claim caps this criterion at 2. Multiple phantom claims (2+) cap it at 1. Vague/hedged language ("some experts say...") without any source caps it at 2 if it functions as an assertion.
Ratings:
- 1 (Not grounded): the agent fabricates most of the substantive content, or contradicts the sources.
- 2 (Largely ungrounded): multiple unsupported claims or one clear invented specific (fake entity/number/URL/quote/tool result).
- 3 (Partially grounded): some claims supported, others plausibly-worded but not actually backed by sources.
- 4 (Mostly grounded): one minor unsupported inference/embellishment that does not materially change the answer; core claims all supported.
- 5 (Fully grounded): every factual claim in every agent turn is directly supported by the grounding sources. No unsupported statements. No invented entities/numbers/dates/URLs/quotes.
Skip (score=null, status="skipped") if: the conversation contains no substantive factual claims to verify (pure chit-chat, refusals, meta-instructions), or has no user turn at all.

### 6. task_completion (score: 1 or 0)
Was the user's overall task successfully and completely accomplished by the END of the conversation? Focus on the FINAL outcome across all turns. Do not assume success just because the user says "thanks".
Ratings:
- 0 (FALSE): one or more parts of the task were not completed, an incorrect/incomplete result was produced, or the goal was left unresolved.
- 1 (TRUE): the agent delivered a complete, correct result that fulfills the user's entire cumulative goal (including refinements added in later turns). The user needs no further action to obtain the outcome they asked for.
Skip (score=null, status="skipped") if: the conversation has no user turn at all.

FAILED TURN
===========
For EACH criterion (except fluency, which is not turn-localized), identify the `failed_turn`: the 0-based index of the EARLIEST user turn whose handling genuinely FAILED for that criterion — i.e., the turn where the agent's response, claims, alignment, or execution actually went wrong.
- Report a turn ONLY when that turn genuinely failed. Do NOT report the merely "weakest" turn if it still succeeded.
- Set `failed_turn` to null whenever: task_adherence score is 1, task_completion score is 1, coherence score is 3, 4, or 5, intent_resolution score is 3, 4, or 5, groundedness score is 3, 4, or 5, or the criterion is skipped.
- Otherwise (coherence, intent_resolution, or groundedness score of 1 or 2, or task_adherence or task_completion score of 0), set `failed_turn` to the 0-based index of the earliest turn that genuinely failed.
- When multiple turns failed, report the EARLIEST failing turn.
- `fluency` always reports `failed_turn: null` (language quality is judged holistically across all agent turns, not localized to one).

OUTPUT FORMAT
=============
Output a single JSON object EXACTLY in this shape (no extra keys, no prose outside the JSON). In each criterion's object, "reason" comes BEFORE "score" — write the reason first (your brief chain-of-thought justification) and only then commit to the score it supports:
{
  "fluency": {
    "reason": "<15-50 words on the agent's language quality across all its turns>",
    "status": "completed" or "skipped",
    "score": <5|4|3|2|1 or null if skipped>,
    "failed_turn": null
  },
  "coherence": {
    "reason": "<15-50 words on the agent's overall coherence across turns>",
    "status": "completed" or "skipped",
    "score": <5|4|3|2|1 or null if skipped>,
    "failed_turn": <0-based earliest failing user turn, or null>
  },
  "intent_resolution": {
    "reason": "<15-50 words on whether the agent correctly understood and took targeted steps toward each turn's intent>",
    "status": "completed" or "skipped",
    "score": <5|4|3|2|1 or null if skipped>,
    "failed_turn": <0-based earliest failing user turn, or null>
  },
  "task_adherence": {
    "reason": "<15-50 words on role/policy compliance across the conversation>",
    "status": "completed" or "skipped",
    "score": <1 or 0 or null if skipped>,
    "failed_turn": <0-based earliest failing user turn, or null if score is 1>
  },
  "groundedness": {
    "reason": "<15-50 words citing which claims were/weren't supported; explicitly note any phantom claims>",
    "status": "completed" or "skipped",
    "score": <5|4|3|2|1 or null if skipped>,
    "failed_turn": <0-based earliest failing user turn, or null>
  },
  "task_completion": {
    "reason": "<15-50 words on whether the final outcome fulfills the cumulative user request>",
    "status": "completed" or "skipped",
    "score": <1 or 0 or null if skipped>,
    "failed_turn": <0-based earliest failing user turn, or null if score is 1>
  }
}

# INPUT
CONVERSATION: {{messages}}

{% if tool_definitions %}
TOOL_DEFINITIONS: {{tool_definitions}}
{% endif %}

CONVERSATION is the full multi-turn dialogue: system message, user queries, assistant text, assistant tool calls ([TOOL_CALL] name(args)), and tool results ([TOOL_RESULT] ...). Tool calls and results are the agent's internal working process; the user only sees the agent's text responses. TOOL_DEFINITIONS lists the tools available to the agent. Some conversations carry NO user turn at all (response-only data used to isolate language quality) — in that case judge fluency only and skip the other five criteria per their own skip conditions below.

# OUTPUT
